The World Bank and International Monetary Fund were established at the Bretton Woods Conference in 1944, nearly five decades after Dr. José Rizal’s execution in 1896 ([Rizal Day](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rizal_Day)), and Ferdinand Marcos—then a young lawyer—had no documented role in the conference ([Bretton Woods Conference](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bretton_Woods_Conference)). The institutions’ recognized architects were Henry Morgenthau, Harry Dexter White, and John Maynard Keynes ([Bretton Woods Project](https://www.brettonwoodsproject.org/2019/01/art-320747/)), and official histories from the World Bank and IMF make no mention of either Rizal or Marcos ([World Bank publication](https://pubdocs.worldbank.org/en/683991541019930363/70-years-chapter-one.pdf)). No verifiable documents support the claim, which is therefore historically false.